  "text": "Here's the complete implementation of the `remove_adjacent_duplicates` function along with the provided unit tests:\n\n```python\nimport unittest\n\ndef remove_adjacent_duplicates(lst):\n    if not lst:\n        return []\n    \n    result = [lst[0]]  # Start with the first element\n    for num in lst[1:]:\n        if num != result[-1]:\n            result.append(num)\n    return result\n\nclass TestRemoveAdjacentDuplicates(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_basic_functionality(self):\n        self.assertEqual(remove_adjacent_duplicates([1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3, 4]), [1, 2, 3, 4])\n    \n    def test_repeated_elements(self):\n        self.assertEqual(remove_adjacent_duplicates([5, 5, 5, 6, 7, 7]), [5, 6, 7])\n    \n    def test_multiple_duplicates(self):\n        self.assertEqual(remove_adjacent_duplicates([1, 2, 3, 4, 4, 4, 4, 5, 5]), [1, 2, 3, 4, 5])\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    unittest.main()\n```\n\n### How It Works:\n- The function starts by checking if the list is empty. If it is, it returns an empty list.\n- It initializes a new list `result` with the first element of the input list.\n- Then it iterates through the rest of the list, adding each element to `result` **only if** it is different from the last element in `result`.\n- This ensures that consecutive duplicates are removed.\n\n### Example:\n```python\nremove_adjacent_duplicates([1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3, 4])  # Output: [1, 2, 3, 4]\nremove_adjacent_duplicates([5, 5, 5, 6, 7, 7])     # Output: [5, 6, 7]\n```\n\n### Running the Tests:\nThe `unittest.main()` will automatically discover and run the test cases if you run the script.",
